WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.180
Australia built a 1907 fence across Western Australia to stop rabbits from spreading, yet the

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:09.340
animals had already crossed before the last wire was strong.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:13.700
This is unintended consequences, episode 120.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:19.900
Crews in Western Australia began unrolling wire and sinking posts in 1907.

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:23.820
They hoped the line would prove impassable to advancing rabbits.

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:28.380
benefits. The plan aimed to keep the animals from reaching richer agricultural districts

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:33.860
further west. By the time the final sections were completed, the rabbits had already moved

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:38.740
beyond the root the fence would follow. The barrier therefore never met the animals it

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:39.800
was meant to turn back.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:45.860
State Agricultural Authorities championed the fence. They faced a rapidly expanding rabbit

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:51.160
population that had been introduced decades earlier for hunting and food. By the early

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:56.280
Early 20th century, those animals were stripping vegetation across vast stretches of the continent.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:00.980
The spread threatened sheep stations and wheat farms that supported the young economy.

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:07.020
Officials reason that a continuous physical barrier maintained by regular patrols could

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:08.620
halt further westward movement.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:12.560
The barrier would buy time for other control measures to take hold.

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:18.460
The approach drew on earlier, smaller fences that had shown some success on individual properties.

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:21.940
At the time, the engineering tasks seemed manageable.

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:26.160
A single long line appeared preferable to endless local enclosures.

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:29.900
Decision makers worked with the tools and understanding available.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:35.600
They viewed the fence as a practical, large-scale application of existing boundary technology.

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:42.380
Construction of the Western Australian barrier began in 1907 and continued for several years.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:47.120
Government crews extended the fence across hundreds of kilometers of arid country.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:52.340
Early reports noted that sections already in place appear to slow rabbit movement in

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:53.000
the short term.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:57.960
Local landholders expressed relief that a coordinated effort was finally underway.

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:02.680
Proponents argued that consistent maintenance and dog patrols would keep the structure

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:03.240
effective.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:08.480
A few observers raised questions about whether the rabbits might already be present on both

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:09.720
sides of the planned route.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:14.620
The prevailing view held that the barrier would still limit further spread once finished.

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:19.360
The project proceeded on the assumption that the animal's advance was a spatial problem

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:21.920
that could be solved by blocking their path.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:27.400
Even when construction crews encountered evidences of fresh diggings or tracks on the western

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:30.600
side of the alignment, the interpretation remained the same.

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:35.900
These were treated as small incursions that the finished fence plus regular patrols could

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:36.600
still contain.

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:40.460
Materials were chosen for durability in dry conditions.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:44.800
Galvanized wire netting was buried at the base to stop burrowing.

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:49.780
The rabbits had in fact crossed the future fence line before construction crews reached

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:50.480
those areas.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:56.300
The completed barrier therefore encountered populations already established on the western side.

00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:01.860
Once the animals were present on both sides, the fence could no longer perform its intended

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:03.080
function of containment.

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:09.480
Later, when mixoma virus was introduced as a biological control, rabbit numbers fell

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:15.640
sharply at first. Over successive seasons however, genetic resistance increased within the surviving

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:21.920
populations. Densities were therefore able to rebound. Because the virus and the rabbits continued

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:26.660
to interact, new strains of the pathogen had to be developed and released repeatedly.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:32.860
The original physical fence, fixed in place and unable to adapt, offered no help against these

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:38.980
changing biological dynamics. Control efforts shifted almost entirely to ongoing biological

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:40.520
and chemical interventions.

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:43.320
The costly barrier remained in the landscape

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:44.820
with little ongoing purpose.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:46.820
Each time a virus strain

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:48.600
killed a large fraction of rabbits,

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:50.460
the survivors carried genes

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:52.100
that conferred partial immunity.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:55.400
They're offspring inherited and amplified those genes.

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:57.440
Within a few breeding seasons,

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:00.000
the proportion of resistant animals rose enough

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.260
that the same virus strain produced only modest mortality.

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:05.740
Field workers then faced the choice

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:08.940
of accepting lower kill rates or investing in new strains.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:14.520
The fence, built of wire and posts, could not be updated in any comparable way.

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:19.960
Its physical presence continued, yet it no longer intersected with the actual movement

00:04:19.960 --> 00:04:23.620
or survival patterns of the population it had been meant to restrain.

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:29.160
Once the limits of the fence became clear, authorities accepted that it would not serve

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:30.380
as a permanent solution.

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:36.700
They gradually reduced emphasis on its maintenance for rabbit control, attention turned to successive

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:42.420
refinements of the mixoma virus and later to additional agents such as rabbit hemorrhagic

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:42.860
disease.

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:48.720
Each new biocontrol measure produced temporary reductions followed by the emergence of resistance.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:53.020
The fence itself has remained a visible feature in parts of the landscape.

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:56.100
Its role in rabbit management is now minimal.

00:04:56.100 --> 00:05:01.920
Modern efforts combine multiple evolving tools rather than relying on any single fixed

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:02.320
barrier.

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:08.080
Interventions that assume a fixed problem can be solved by a fixed structure often meet

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:10.360
systems capable of changing around them.

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:16.520
When the target is a living population, adaptation can render spatial barriers irrelevant once

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:19.180
the organisms are already present on both sides.

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:24.280
Planners today can ask whether an intervention will still function after the first round

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:30.160
response from the system it aims to affect. In fields from public health to technology regulation,

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:35.120
the same pattern appears whenever solutions remain static while the underlying challenge

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:40.820
continues to evolve. Consider current efforts to secure digital networks with static perimeter

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:46.680
defenses, while threats adapt through code that evolves daily. What indicators would you watch

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:51.520
to see whether a chosen barrier is already being outflanked by the next generation of change?

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:54.220
And that's where today's story leaves us.

